Usage: worktree acquire --issue N [--session S] [--base REF] [--reuse] [--isolate] [--no-sync] [--format json]

Acquire (or reuse) a registered worktree directory for the issue and record the caller's session; the session comes from --session or PHASEGENT_SESSION_ID and must be explicit. Idempotent: re-running with the same repository, issue, and session returns the same directory_id (reason="idempotent"). The default is reuse-current-unless-the-target-path-is-occupied: the current checkout is reused (reason="no_conflict") whether it is clean, dirty, unbound, or linked to a historical issue. Fresh occupancy reported by the connected service, or an associated task that is still open, protects the path; a confirmed closed historical task does not veto reuse by itself. An unresolved occupation of the checkout path is reported as an `isolation` error naming this command with `--isolate`; the command never creates a worktree unless `--isolate` was passed explicitly, in which case it allocates a fresh `phasegent/<issue>-<short6hex>` branch and a new worktree under ~/.cache/phasegent/worktrees/<fingerprint>/<slug> (reason="new_worktree"). Dirty state is advisory only: it warns on stderr but never forces isolation, never stashes, and never mutates branches. `--reuse` states the reuse preference explicitly and `--isolate` forces a fresh worktree; the two are mutually exclusive, and `--reuse` never bypasses an occupied checkout. `--base REF` only selects the start point of the explicit `--isolate` worktree; without `--isolate` it is rejected and never creates one. When the `git status` probe itself fails the dirty state is unknown: `--isolate` creates a fresh worktree, otherwise the current checkout is reused, and both emit a stderr warning — an unknown status is never silently treated as clean. On every successful acquire the managed commit hooks are installed when the acquired checkout has a git origin, so one command leaves the checkout ready; the install reuses the standard hook helper and degrades to a warning that never fails the acquire. A provider-scoped Forgejo or GitLab acquisition writes the durable task bound to the issue in this repository and refuses before any registry write or taxi pass when the stored task contradicts the selected scope; task ownership applies for every provider and without repeating --provider. The command never writes branch/issue ownership outside the registry. Authorized Operator/orchestrator callers run the repository's issue sync pass first and forward its warnings to stderr; --no-sync skips it. Orchestrator-only for agents; ordinary human Operator shares this authority, managed sessions without a role fail closed. See docs/worktree-maintenance.md and docs/cli-caller-context.md.
